President Trump has declared a national  emergency at the US border with Mexico  mr. Trump says this allows him to get  billions of dollars that Congress  refused to give him to build a wall  there but the president is facing sharp  criticism about his decision and he says  he expects a long legal battle and we  will then be sued and they will sue us  in the Ninth Circuit even though it  shouldn't be there and we will possibly  get a bad ruling and then we'll get  another bad ruling and then we'll end up  in the Supreme Court and hopefully we'll  get a fair shake and we'll win in the  Supreme Court president Trump says the  wall is needed to stop what he claims is  a flow of drugs criminals and illegal  immigrants coming into the country here  to talk more about that is Caitlin  Dickerson immigration reporter for The  New York Times Caitlin welcome thank you  so is this more political emergency or a  national emergency because it took  several weeks if not months and years in  office for the President to declare this  a national emergency I think it depends  how you look at this issue president  Trump and many of his supporters clearly  do see it as an emergency but I don't  think when they say that they mean  something new as you pointed out  immigration is is not a new concept at  all and actually illegal immigration to  the country is down but the fact is that  President Trump campaigned on this  promise to build a border wall it's one  of the most popular things that he said  and that's why you hear him repeat it so  often and that's why he was really boxed  into a corner right because as much as  he felt the need to deliver on this  promise he found that it became very  difficult to get Congress to support it  and Congress controls the power of the  purse so he was really boxed in in terms  of how to move forward and I think  that's how we got to where we are today  the national emergency was really the  only way that he could describe this  issue and could use this issue legally  in order to get it paid for so you've  actually been to one of those border  cities El Paso recently what can you  tell us about what we're you're really  seeing there on the ground sure so just  to situate people El Paso is on the  border in West Texas is right below New  Mexico and it's a very immigrant heavy  it's a it's a very highly trap  back to port it's very busy and I think  it's one of those classic border cities  that you hear about quite often where  people move back and forth along the  border every day you know my uber driver  one day told me he lives in Ciudad  Juarez in Mexico because he it's more  affordable and then he crosses the  border every day to work are you seeing  any of what the president is talking  about or describing no I mean I would  say one of the most surprising things  maybe maybe the most surprising thing  about President Trump State of the Union  address was this idea he put forth that  El Paso had been one of the dangerous  most dangerous cities in the country  before a wall was constructed there a  fence was constructed there about a  decade ago it's just not true and you  just don't see it borne out every day  it's a very safe city I mean let's not  pretend that there's no impact on El  Paso from immigration it's true that  when people are processed through the  immigration system and they're released  there's an effect on the local  healthcare system for a few days  typically then people move on to their  destinations within the US but really  what I saw more than anything was a safe  city one that's been that way for  decades and one that really rallies  around the immigrant community so the  sort of volunteer work that's needed to  help support the new immigrants there's  no shortage of in Congress did pass a  bipartisan agreement a border security  package not money for a wall but but  what is in there  so the border security package I think  one of the reasons that you saw Congress  be able to come together in a rare  instance and pass it is that it's not  terribly controversial or surprising you  saw some additional money for the Border  Patrol facilities that are in remote  areas those are the types of facilities  that the two young migrant children have  passed through during Christmastime who  died and I think those those deaths drew  a lot of you know needed attention to  those facilities that needed some  support in addition to that you see some  more money for health care and  infrastructure and you see money for  additional ice detention beds so I think  that was probably one of the most  contentious things is that well we've  all been focused on the border and  asylum in the wall interior enforcement  those interior arrests have continued to  go really strong some that it's not  getting reported as often and I think  it's because we're inundated I think you  know the public isn't inundated and  quite frankly journalists are inundated  because that so much has changed I  covered immigration for years and this  has just been an incredibly busy period  where it becomes difficult to cover  everything with the degree of you know  urgency that it requires Caitlyn  Dickerson thank you so much brilliant  points made thank you so much
 

